/-
# Graphplay.Dowsing.NoiseEquitable

**Hole D8 — Equitable-symmetric noise preservation, formally.**

`Graphplay.Toolkit.Noise` (file A8) introduces the structures
`NoiseModel`, `cellProjector`, `cellUniformSymmetric`, and the deferred
`NoiseModel.quotient`, together with a statement-level theorem
`cellUniform_preserved`.  All of these are statement-only scaffolding.

This Dowsing file pushes the theory by addressing two complementary questions
left open in A8.

**Q1 (preservation).**  *Which* noise models automatically preserve every
equitable partition of every Hermitian host graph?  We give a clean
commutant-based criterion (every Lindblad `L_k` must live in the **commutant
of the partition algebra** — equivalently, must commute with the
cell-projector), prove the easy direction, and characterise the *universal*
case (preserves every equitable partition of every host) as the centre of
`Mat_n(ℂ)`, i.e. the scalar multiples of the identity.

**Q2 (noise-assisted speedup, Caruso et al.).**  When noise *breaks* the
partition's symmetry, *some* dephasing channels can lift accidental
degeneracies and improve search / transport probabilities.  We define a
**breaking score** as the (L²) distance of `N`'s commutant from the
projector algebra of `P`, and state the Caruso conjecture: optimal
noise-assisted speedup occurs at a non-trivial breaking score (strictly
between fully symmetric and fully disruptive).

Concrete models analysed: depolarising (universally equitable), site
dephasing (equitable iff cells are vertex-orbits), boundary dephasing (not
equitable for marked-refined partitions; explicit breaking score).

References inside `references/`:
* `2110.13686.txt` — Bick & Sclosa, *Dynamical Systems on Graph Limits and
  their Symmetries*.  Symmetry-induced invariant subspaces (Sec. 2.1, 3.4)
  and **ghost-of-symmetry** phenomena (Sec. 6) are the dynamical-systems
  analogue of the operator-algebraic story below.
* `1108.0339.txt` — Bachman & Tamon's PST reduction: closed-system PST on a
  graph iff PST on the equitable quotient.  We state and conjecture its
  open-system extension.

All cell-level proofs are deferred via `sorry`.  Statements compile against
the existing `Graphplay.WeightedGraph`, `EquitablePartition`, and
`NoiseModel` types.
-/

